        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-12-03T15:58:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This is a larger question about how threading is handled in the GLES backend, but how is the context handled when another Recorder thread tries to create a DawnBuffer or DawnTexture? Do we switch thread the GL context is current on?",
      "parentUuid": "16140c7f_5f901d47",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 241,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 241,
        "endChar": 34
      },
      "revId": "e8bbd53e280e46138717bd5396841a559a123c2b",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ed4176dd_a6f4202c",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/dawn/DawnBuffer.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 241,
      "author": {
        "id": 19434
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-12-03T16:22:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e how is the context handled when another Recorder thread tries to create a DawnBuffer or DawnTexture? \n\nWe would defer the creations of GL buffers/GL textures until `Queue::submit` is called. This is one approach to keep all GL calls on the playback thread. This is one direction we plan to go in Dawn/GLES for both Clank \u0026 WebView. And Stephen would prefer to avoid shadow copies \u0026 context switching between threads in this approach.\n\nAnw, my suggestion for moving map to `QueueManager::onSubmitToGpu` might not be necessary in this CL. It could be done separately. The suggestion is only for making sure the deferred approach can work 100% and no shadow copies, no context switching are needed.",
      "parentUuid": "27963857_944004e3",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 241,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 241,
        "endChar": 34
      },
      "revId": "e8bbd53e280e46138717bd5396841a559a123c2b",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "cab69e95_5db27ae1",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/dawn/DawnBuffer.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 241,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-12-03T16:25:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If the GL buffer isn\u0027t being created until `Queue::submit`, then by definition the Recorder on the viz compositor thread will need to have shadow copies because we write the data during or before `Recorder::snap()`. This would require the DawnBuffer to provide a CPU buffer we write to that it is pretending is the \"mapped\" buffer and then memcpy it during submit().",
      "parentUuid": "ed4176dd_a6f4202c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 241,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 241,
        "endChar": 34
      },
      "revId": "e8bbd53e280e46138717bd5396841a559a123c2b",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4f5169f2_86dad278",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/dawn/DawnBuffer.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 241,
      "author": {
        "id": 19434
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-12-03T16:32:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, a shadow copy before 1st \"real\" map in Dawn/GLES is acceptable.",
      "parentUuid": "cab69e95_5db27ae1",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 241,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 241,
        "endChar": 34
      },
      "revId": "e8bbd53e280e46138717bd5396841a559a123c2b",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9ed36ab6_b9d13b47",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/dawn/DawnBuffer.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 241,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-12-03T16:49:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "At a high-level, I\u0027m open to changing how the DawnBuffer mapping flow works if it leads to a simpler implementation in Graphite. But I don\u0027t think (for the reasons Greg has outlined), relying on Graphite to hold things in exactly the right way to meet Dawn-GLES\u0027s threading requirements is a good path forward. Graphite should operate on capabilities that Dawn exposes or Dawn should hide backend differences.\n\nSo viable paths forward for handling multithreaded data writing for GLES in Dawn that I think could work with these constraints are:\n\n1. Dawn exposes a capability for multithreaded resource creation and Graphite takes over all the CPU shadow copy responsibilities and handles creating DawnBuffers and DawnTextures only on submit(). It\u0027s still brittle in the sense that Chrome has to then use the Context on the GL main thread.\n\n2. Dawn-GLES handles shadow copies and other multithreaded resource creation as efficiently as possible and hides the details from Graphite. I believe this can be done in a way that is competitive with Ganesh:\n * Use a shared GL context for creating and uploading texture data off of the main thread if possible.\n * Use shadow copies buffers created off the GL thread if they\u0027ve never been mapped before\n * Use persistent mapping to preserve the mapped pointer after the first map, or automatically re-map writable buffers at the end of a command buffer submission so that it\u0027s address is visible on other threads w/o needing to switch the context.\n * Resource API calls that happen to be made on the GL thread can operate directly w/o needing to use shadow copies.\n \nAs long as dawn can switch between shadow copy and direct GL access on the main thread, it\u0027ll be no different from how Ganesh operates with SkDDLs right now. Ganesh explicitly manages CPU shadow copies of all of its buffers that are created on the viz thread.",
      "parentUuid": "4f5169f2_86dad278",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 241,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 241,
        "endChar": 34
      },
      "revId": "e8bbd53e280e46138717bd5396841a559a123c2b",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
